("(faker's `seed`) to remain deterministic. ⚠ Verify current APIs of these libraries.","(faker's `faker.seed(n)`) to remain deterministic. Check each library's docs for its current API."),
("- **Relative paths** (`/api/users/:id`) are resolved against `location.href` **when a `location` exists** (browser, or jsdom). ⚠ In Node, however, **`fetch(\"/api/users/1\")` itself throws** because Node's `fetch` requires an absolute URL (there is no base). In a jsdom Vitest environment, global `fetch` is typically Node's `fetch`, not jsdom's, so relative request URLs fail *before* MSW sees them. Use an absolute API base URL in tests: read it from `import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL` (set in `.env.test` or `vitest.config.ts` `test.env`), and build handlers from the same constant. ⚠ Behavior varies by environment/version; verify.",
 "- **Relative paths** (`/api/users/:id`) are resolved against `location.href` **when a `location` exists** (browser, or jsdom). *Verified on MSW 2.15.0 + Vitest 3.2.7 + jsdom 30 + Node 24:* with `environment: \"jsdom\"` and the server listening, `fetch(\"/api/rel\")` is intercepted and matched by `http.get(\"/api/rel\", …)` (it resolves against `http://localhost:3000/`); an unmatched relative request is reported by `onUnhandledRequest: \"error\"`. Without MSW, or in `environment: \"node\"` (no `location`), `fetch(\"/api/1\")` throws `TypeError: Failed to parse URL from /api/1`, because Node's `fetch` needs an absolute URL. So relative URLs work in jsdom component tests only while MSW is active; for portability (node-environment tests, SSR, unit tests of the API client) use an absolute API base URL read from `import.meta.env.VITE_API_URL` (set in `.env.test` or `vitest.config.ts` `test.env`), and build handlers from the same constant."),
("`fetch` rejects with `AbortError`; the handler may still run (the response is discarded). ⚠ Behavior of aborting in-flight mocked requests varies by interceptor version; verify before asserting that a handler *did not complete*.",
 "`fetch` rejects with `AbortError`; the handler may still run (the response is discarded). *Verified in `environment: \"node\"` on MSW 2.15.0:* `fetch` rejected with `AbortError: This operation was aborted` and the handler still ran to completion (`completed === true`). Do not assert that a handler *did not complete*; assert on the client's behavior instead. (Under `jsdom` this test fails earlier with an `AbortSignal` mismatch, see §10.3.)"),
("⚠ `mockServiceWorker.js` must **never** ship enabled in production:","`mockServiceWorker.js` must **never** ship enabled in production:"),
("The second test shows the safety net: an unmatched request produces an error rather than a silent network call. ⚠ The exact error type/message differs by version; assert only on rejection.","The second test shows the safety net: an unmatched request produces an error rather than a silent network call. *Verified on MSW 2.15.0:* `fetch` rejects (the message begins `[MSW] Cannot bypass a request when using the \"error\" strategy…`) and MSW logs `[MSW] Error: intercepted a request without a matching request handler` to `console.error`. The exact error type/message differs by version (and the option is renamed `onUnhandledFrame` in MSW 3); assert only on rejection."),
("`http.post<Params, RequestBody, ResponseBody>` generics (⚠ the generic parameter order and constraints must be verified for your MSW version; `never` means \"no params\").","`http.post<Params, RequestBody, ResponseBody>` generics (order verified: this exact handler type-checks under `tsc --strict` against msw 2.15.0; `never` means \"no params\")."),
